# Apple client source selection - ONE repository, ONE source, TWO products.
#
# The two Apple products this fork ships are built from the SAME Swift source tree, at
# the SAME commit, with the SAME Libbox framework:
#
#   iOS / SFI     scheme SFI   -> one client source SHA
#   macOS / SFM   scheme SFM   -> the same client source SHA
#
# # What the parent repository decides, and what it does not
#
# The parent decides ONE thing: which Apple source commit this release is built from.
# It does not decide which UI a device shows. That is a runtime property of the Apple
# source itself, which routes presentation from the device idiom:
#
#   iPhone  -> the fork's Hako presentation
#   iPad    -> upstream's presentation
#   macOS   -> upstream's presentation
#
# Because that routing lives in the Apple source, the parent must NOT select a different
# commit per platform. An earlier revision of this file pinned macOS to an independent
# `dev` commit, which meant the two products were built from two different sources and the
# parent - not the Apple source - was deciding UI ownership. That model is gone.
#
# # Where the SHA comes from
#
# `clients/apple` is this repository's submodule, and its gitlink is the single authority:
# it is what the release commit records. A second copy of the same value here could
# disagree with it, so there is none. Read it with:
#
#     git ls-tree HEAD clients/apple
#
# `scripts/ci/apple-client-source.sh sha ios` and `sha macos` both return that gitlink, and
# `assert-shared-source` fails the build if they ever differ.
#
# scripts/ci/apple-client-source.sh is the only reader of this file.

# The single Apple client repository. Used for the submodule URL check and for the
# ephemeral same-SHA working tree a platform overlay needs.
APPLE_CLIENT_REPOSITORY=https://github.com/Piggy-Cat-bit-shadow/sing-box-for-apple

# Informational only, for diagnostics and for the ancestor check that proves the pinned
# commit is on the branch the fork reviews. It never selects which commit is built: the
# gitlink does that, so a branch that moves cannot move a release.
APPLE_CLIENT_BRANCH=main

# The iPhone Hako presentation's design baseline, as a tag rather than a branch.
#
# This replaces `IOS_APPLE_BRANCH=hako-ui`, which named a branch that no longer exists. The Apple
# repository was consolidated to `main` (product line) and `dev` (upstream reference); every
# construction branch was retired and its content kept as a tag. Release metadata must not depend
# on a deleted branch, so the design truth is recorded as the tag that now carries it.
#
# The value is not read by any script. `platform_branch` answers with `APPLE_CLIENT_BRANCH` for
# both platforms and deliberately does not fall back to a per-product branch name; that per-product
# name was already unread, which is why removing it changes no behaviour. Resolve the commit with:
#
#     git -C clients/apple rev-parse archive/hako-design-v1^{commit}
HAKO_DESIGN_BASELINE_TAG=archive/hako-design-v1
